        "---\n",
        "title: \"Vector Space Models\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Document-term matrices, cosine retrieval, SVD, and latent semantic structure\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
        "In the previous chapters, documents were represented by count vectors, TF-IDF vectors, and probabilistic models.  In this chapter we study these representations from a systematic linear-algebraic point of view.  A corpus becomes a matrix, documents become rows, vocabulary terms become columns, similarity becomes an inner product, and latent semantic structure is extracted by projection onto low-dimensional subspaces.\n",
        "\n",
        "The central mathematical tool is the singular value decomposition.  It turns a large sparse document-term matrix into a sequence of best low-rank approximations.  This gives the classical method known as **latent semantic analysis** and also prepares us for modern embedding models.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. represent a corpus as a document-term matrix $X\\in R^{n\\times d}$;\n",
        "2. interpret rows, columns, row space, column space, rank, and Gram matrices in NLP terms;\n",
        "3. compute document similarity using inner products and cosine similarity;\n",
        "4. express retrieval scores in matrix form;\n",
        "5. explain why normalization changes the geometry of document comparison;\n",
        "6. state the singular value decomposition and connect it to eigenvalue problems;\n",
        "7. use the Eckart--Young--Mirsky theorem to justify low-rank approximation;\n",
        "8. construct latent semantic analysis from a truncated SVD;\n",
        "9. compare raw vector-space retrieval with low-rank latent retrieval;\n",
        "10. implement the main ideas in Python using NumPy;\n",
        "11. use Plotly.js to visualize latent document geometry;\n",
        "12. use AI tools to critique representation choices, identify hidden assumptions, and explain model behavior.\n",
        "\n",
        "Throughout this chapter, $R$ denotes the real numbers.  In code we use ordinary NumPy arrays, but the mathematical object is a matrix representation of a finite corpus.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The main object: a document-term matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D=\\{d_1,d_2,\\ldots,d_n\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be a corpus of $n$ documents, and let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V=\\{v_1,v_2,\\ldots,v_d\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be a fixed vocabulary of $d$ tokens.  A vector-space model chooses a representation map\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi:D\\longrightarrow R^d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The $i$th document is represented by\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x_i=\\phi(d_i)=(x_{i1},x_{i2},\\ldots,x_{id})\\in R^d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The full corpus is represented by the matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "--- & x_1^T & ---\\\\\n",
        "--- & x_2^T & ---\\\\\n",
        "& \\vdots & \\\\\n",
        "--- & x_n^T & ---\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\in R^{n\\times d}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The entry $X_{ij}$ is the weight of term $v_j$ in document $d_i$.  Depending on the representation, $X_{ij}$ may be a count, a binary indicator, a relative frequency, or a TF-IDF weight.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Document-term matrix.**  A corpus is converted into a matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X\\in R^{n\\times d},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where rows index documents and columns index vocabulary terms.  Most classical NLP methods can be described as operations on this matrix.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "This viewpoint is powerful because it reduces many NLP operations to familiar linear algebra:\n",
        "\n",
        "| NLP operation | Linear algebra operation |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| represent a document | choose a row vector $x_i\\in R^d$ |\n",
        "| compare two documents | compute $x_i^T x_j$ or cosine similarity |\n",
        "| compare all documents | compute the Gram matrix $XX^T$ |\n",
        "| compare all terms | compute the Gram matrix $X^TX$ |\n",
        "| remove unimportant dimensions | project onto a subspace |\n",
        "| find latent topics | compute a low-rank approximation |\n",
        "| search with a query | compute matrix-vector scores $Xq$ |\n",
        "\n",
        "## Rows, columns, and geometry\n",
        "\n",
        "The row vector $x_i\\in R^d$ describes document $d_i$ as a point in vocabulary space.  The $j$th column\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X_{:j}=\\begin{bmatrix}X_{1j}\\\\X_{2j}\\\\\\vdots\\\\X_{nj}\\end{bmatrix}\\in R^n\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "describes how term $v_j$ is distributed across documents.\n",
        "\n",
        "These two perspectives are dual:\n",
        "\n",
        "- rows describe **documents by terms**;\n",
        "- columns describe **terms by documents**.\n",
        "\n",
        "The document Gram matrix is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "G_D=XX^T\\in R^{n\\times n},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "with entries\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(G_D)_{ij}=x_i^Tx_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The term Gram matrix is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "G_T=X^TX\\in R^{d\\times d},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "with entries\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(G_T)_{jk}=X_{:j}^T X_{:k}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus $G_D$ measures document-document similarity, while $G_T$ measures term-term co-occurrence similarity.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Two Gram matrices.**\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "XX^T \\quad \\text{compares documents},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "while\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X^TX \\quad \\text{compares terms}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The singular value decomposition links these two matrices through the same singular values.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Inner product retrieval\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a query is represented by a vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "q\\in R^d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple retrieval score for document $d_i$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s_i=x_i^Tq.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For the whole corpus, the score vector is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s=Xq\\in R^n.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Documents with larger scores are considered more relevant.  This is the foundation of classical vector-space search.\n",
        "\n",
        "The inner product has a clear meaning for nonnegative text vectors:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x_i^Tq=\\sum_{j=1}^d x_{ij}q_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Only terms that appear in both the document and the query contribute positively.  Large weights produce larger scores.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, raw inner products favor long documents because long documents tend to have larger norms.  To reduce this length bias, we often use cosine similarity.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Cosine similarity and normalized geometry\n",
        "\n",
        "For nonzero vectors $x,y\\in R^d$, the cosine similarity is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{cos}(x,y)=\\frac{x^Ty}{\\lVert x\\rVert_2\\lVert y\\rVert_2}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the inner product after projecting both vectors to the unit sphere:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat x=\\frac{x}{\\lVert x\\rVert_2},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\widehat y=\\frac{y}{\\lVert y\\rVert_2}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{cos}(x,y)=\\widehat x^T\\widehat y.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Cosine similarity compares direction rather than magnitude.  In document analysis, direction often represents relative term usage, while magnitude is partly influenced by document length.\n",
        "\n",
        "If $\\widetilde X$ is the row-normalized document matrix, then all pairwise cosine similarities are contained in\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "C=\\widetilde X\\widetilde X^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Similarly, query scores are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s=\\widetilde X\\widehat q.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Normalization changes the model.**  Raw inner products ask: \"How much weighted vocabulary overlap is there?\"  Cosine similarity asks: \"How similar are the directions of the two term-weight vectors?\"  These are different statistical assumptions about what should count as similarity.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Python example: building a document-term matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "The following example uses a small corpus.  The goal is not to build a production search engine, but to expose every mathematical step."
      ],
      "id": "e97e1101"
    },
