    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The only change is the geometry of the feature space.  The similarity calculation is still cosine similarity, but the axes have been rescaled.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Rank and latent structure\n",
        "\n",
        "The rank of $X$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{rank}(X)=\\dim(\\operatorname{Row}(X))=\\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(X)).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In NLP, a high-dimensional vocabulary space often contains redundancy:\n",
        "\n",
        "- different words may carry related meanings;\n",
        "- topics create correlations among terms;\n",
        "- document collections often lie near lower-dimensional structures;\n",
        "- noise and rare words inflate the apparent dimension.\n",
        "\n",
        "A low-rank approximation tries to replace $X$ by a simpler matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X_k\\in R^{n\\times d},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\operatorname{rank}(X_k)\\le k,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $k\\ll \\min(n,d)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The goal is to keep the most important geometric structure while discarding high-dimensional noise.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Singular value decomposition\n",
        "\n",
        "Every matrix $X\\in R^{n\\times d}$ has a singular value decomposition\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X=U\\Sigma V^T,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $U\\in R^{n\\times r}$ has orthonormal columns;\n",
        "- $V\\in R^{d\\times r}$ has orthonormal columns;\n",
        "- $\\Sigma=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_r)$;\n",
        "- $r=\\operatorname{rank}(X)$;\n",
        "- $\\sigma_1\\ge \\sigma_2\\ge \\cdots\\ge \\sigma_r>0$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The columns of $U$ are left singular vectors.  The columns of $V$ are right singular vectors.  The singular values measure how much variation lies in each singular direction.\n",
        "\n",
        "The SVD diagonalizes the two Gram matrices:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "XX^T=U\\Sigma^2U^T,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X^TX=V\\Sigma^2V^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus left singular vectors describe principal directions among documents, while right singular vectors describe principal directions among terms.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Best low-rank approximation\n",
        "\n",
        "For $k<r$, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "U_k=[u_1,\\ldots,u_k],\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "V_k=[v_1,\\ldots,v_k],\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\Sigma_k=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_k).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The rank-$k$ truncated SVD approximation is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X_k=U_k\\Sigma_kV_k^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The Eckart--Young--Mirsky theorem says that $X_k$ is the best rank-$k$ approximation to $X$ in Frobenius norm:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X_k=\\arg\\min_{\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le k}\\lVert X-A\\rVert_F.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Moreover,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\lVert X-X_k\\rVert_F^2=\\sum_{j=k+1}^r \\sigma_j^2.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This theorem is the mathematical foundation of latent semantic analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**SVD interpretation for NLP.**  The truncated SVD replaces sparse surface-level term matching by a lower-dimensional coordinate system.  Documents that do not share many exact words may become close if they have similar coordinates in the latent space.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Latent semantic analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "Latent semantic analysis, or LSA, applies truncated SVD to a term-weighted document matrix such as $X^{\\operatorname{tfidf}}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X\\approx X_k=U_k\\Sigma_kV_k^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The row vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_i=(U_k\\Sigma_k)_{i:}\\in R^k\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "is a $k$-dimensional latent representation of document $d_i$.  The row vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "t_j=(V_k\\Sigma_k)_{j:}\\in R^k\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "may be interpreted as a latent representation of term $v_j$.\n",
        "\n",
        "A query vector $q\\in R^d$ can be projected into the same latent coordinate system by\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_q=q^TV_k\\in R^k.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then retrieval can be performed by comparing $z_q$ with the document coordinates $z_i$.\n",
        "\n",
        "There are several conventions for scaling latent coordinates.  The important point is that documents and queries are compared after projection onto the subspace spanned by the top right singular vectors.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Python example: SVD and low-rank coordinates"
