      "source": [
        "Low-rank retrieval may retrieve documents that do not share many exact query words but lie in a related latent direction.  This is the main advantage of LSA over purely surface-level matching.\n",
        "\n",
        "## How LSA handles synonymy and polysemy\n",
        "\n",
        "Two common language difficulties are **synonymy** and **polysemy**.\n",
        "\n",
        "Synonymy means that different words have related meanings.  For example, \"car\" and \"automobile\" may not match exactly in a count-vector model.  If they occur in similar document contexts, a low-rank model may place them near each other.\n",
        "\n",
        "Polysemy means that one word has multiple meanings.  For example, \"bank\" may refer to a financial institution or a river bank.  A simple vector-space model assigns one column to the token \"bank,\" so it cannot fully separate meanings.  LSA may partially separate contexts through document-level directions, but it still lacks the contextual flexibility of modern neural embeddings.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**What LSA can and cannot do.**  LSA can discover linear correlation structure in a corpus.  It cannot truly understand syntax, word order, or context-dependent meaning.  Its strength is low-rank linear geometry; its limitation is that language is not purely linear bag-of-words geometry.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Centering, scaling, and preprocessing as linear transformations\n",
        "\n",
        "Many preprocessing steps can be described as transformations of $X$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Row normalization\n",
        "\n",
        "Row normalization replaces each document vector by\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat x_i=\\frac{x_i}{\\lVert x_i\\rVert_2}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This maps documents to the unit sphere in $R^d$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Column scaling\n",
        "\n",
        "TF-IDF is column scaling:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X\\mapsto XW.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This changes the metric by giving different importance to different vocabulary dimensions.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Stop-word removal\n",
        "\n",
        "Removing a set of stop-word columns is projection onto a coordinate subspace.  If $S$ is the set of retained columns, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X\\mapsto XP_S,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $P_S$ selects the retained vocabulary coordinates.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Centering\n",
        "\n",
        "Centering subtracts the column mean:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X_c=X-\\mathbf{1}\\mu^T,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mu=\\frac{1}{n}X^T\\mathbf{1}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Centering is standard in PCA, but it must be used carefully in sparse nonnegative text matrices because it destroys sparsity and creates negative entries.\n",
        "\n",
        "## PCA and LSA: same theorem, different data convention\n",
        "\n",
        "PCA is usually applied to centered data.  LSA is usually applied to uncentered TF-IDF data.  Both use the SVD.\n",
        "\n",
        "For centered data $X_c$, PCA finds directions maximizing variance:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "v_1=\\arg\\max_{\\lVert v\\rVert=1}\\lVert X_cv\\rVert_2^2.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The solution is the top right singular vector of $X_c$.\n",
        "\n",
        "LSA instead applies SVD to a sparse term-weight matrix, often without centering:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X^{\\operatorname{tfidf}}=U\\Sigma V^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The purpose is not variance analysis in the classical statistical sense, but low-rank semantic approximation for retrieval and representation.\n",
        "\n",
        "## A statistical interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "A document-term matrix can be viewed as data sampled from an underlying distribution.  Suppose each document is generated from a mixture of latent topics.  Then terms that often occur together across documents produce correlated columns in $X$.\n",
        "\n",
        "A low-rank model assumes that much of this correlation can be explained by a small number of latent factors.  Informally,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X\\approx AB^T,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A\\in R^{n\\times k}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "contains document factors and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "B\\in R^{d\\times k}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "contains term factors.\n",
        "\n",
        "The SVD gives the best unconstrained linear factorization under squared error.  Later models, such as nonnegative matrix factorization and topic models, impose additional constraints or probabilistic assumptions.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Worked hand calculation\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider three documents and four terms:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "1&1&0&0\\\\\n",
        "1&0&1&0\\\\\n",
        "0&0&1&1\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The document Gram matrix is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "XX^T=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "2&1&0\\\\\n",
        "1&2&1\\\\\n",
        "0&1&2\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus documents 1 and 2 overlap in one term, documents 2 and 3 overlap in one term, and documents 1 and 3 do not overlap.  The cosine similarity matrix is obtained by dividing each row and column by document norms.  Since every row has norm $\\sqrt{2}$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "C=\\frac{1}{2}XX^T\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "1&1/2&0\\\\\n",
        "1/2&1&1/2\\\\\n",
        "0&1/2&1\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This example shows why vector-space models can be understood entirely through matrix products.\n",
        "\n",
        "## AI-assisted learning activities\n",
        "\n",
        "AI tools can help students learn vector-space models, but they should be used as mathematical assistants rather than answer machines.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Activity 1: representation audit\n",
        "\n",
        "Give an AI tool the following prompt:\n",
        "\n",
